{"name":"Hot Jazz","hex":"#bc3033","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"hot jazz","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Synonym of Dixieland (“jazz music subgenre”)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hot_jazz"}],"entries":[{"title":"Dixieland jazz","blocks":[{"text":"Dixieland jazz, also referred to as traditional jazz, hot jazz, or simply Dixieland, is a style of jazz based on the music that developed in New Orleans at the start of the 20th century. The 1917 recordings by the Original Dixieland Jass Band fostered awareness of this new style of music."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dixieland_jazz","wikidata":"Q906647"},{"title":"Jazz","blocks":[{"text":"Jazz is a music genre that originated in the African-American communities of New Orleans, Louisiana, in the late 19th and early 20th centuries. Its roots are in blues, ragtime, European harmony, African rhythmic rituals, spirituals, hymns, marches, vaudeville song, and dance music. Since the 1920s Jazz Age, it has been recognized as a major form of musical expression in both traditional and popular music. Jazz is characterized by swing and blue notes, complex chords, call and response vocals, polyrhythms and improvisation."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jazz","wikidata":"Q8341"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/BrianOConnell05.jpg/500px-BrianOConnell05.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/BrianOConnell05.jpg/500px-BrianOConnell05.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A traditionalist jazz band plays at a party in New Orleans in 2005.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABrianOConnell05.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Internationales_Dixieland_Festival_2012_Dresden_8.JPG/500px-Internationales_Dixieland_Festival_20